1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,416
In this lesson, we'll find the average
height of a function over an interval.

2
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:12,028
The average value of f on a to b is one
over b minus a, times the integral from a

3
00:00:12,028 --> 00:00:12,829
to b of f.

4
00:00:13,279 --> 00:00:17,055
Total accumulation divided by the length
of the interval.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,251
Find the average of x squared on zero to
three.

6
00:00:21,701 --> 00:00:26,479
One third times the integral, which is
nine. So the average value is three.

7
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:32,438
Geometrically, it's the height of the
rectangle with the same width and the same

8
00:00:32,438 --> 00:00:34,555
area as the region under the curve.

9
00:00:35,005 --> 00:00:38,333
The extra area above that height fills the
gap below it.

10
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:44,651
If f is continuous, some c in the interval
has f of c equal to the average.

11
00:00:45,101 --> 00:00:50,733
For x squared on zero to three, c squared
equals three, so c equals root three.

12
00:00:51,867 --> 00:00:58,175
The temperature is T of t equals sixty
plus ten sine of pi t over twelve, for t

13
00:00:58,175 --> 00:01:00,251
from zero to twelve hours.

14
00:01:00,701 --> 00:01:06,929
The average is sixty plus ten over twelve
times twenty four over pi, about sixty six

15
00:01:06,929 --> 00:01:08,338
point four degrees.

16
00:01:09,467 --> 00:01:13,755
Your turn. Find the average value of sine
x on zero to pi.

17
00:01:14,205 --> 00:01:15,741
Pause the video and try it.

18
00:01:16,191 --> 00:01:20,841
One over pi times two, which is two over
pi, about point six four.

19
00:01:21,966 --> 00:01:27,171
In this lesson, we'll use integrals to
track motion and other accumulated change.

20
00:01:28,299 --> 00:01:33,889
The integral of velocity from a to b gives
displacement, the net change in position.

21
00:01:34,339 --> 00:01:35,960
Movement backward subtracts.

22
00:01:37,099 --> 00:01:42,185
The integral of speed, the absolute value
of velocity, gives total distance

23
00:01:42,185 --> 00:01:42,795
traveled.

24
00:01:43,245 --> 00:01:49,064
For v of t equals t squared minus four on
zero to three, displacement is negative

25
00:01:49,064 --> 00:01:54,668
three, but distance is sixteen thirds plus
seven thirds, which is twenty three

26
00:01:54,668 --> 00:01:55,171
thirds.

27
00:01:56,299 --> 00:02:01,334
Position at time t is the starting
position plus the integral of velocity.

28
00:02:01,784 --> 00:02:07,878
If s of zero is five and v equals two t,
then s of three is five plus nine, which

29
00:02:07,878 --> 00:02:08,781
is fourteen.

30
00:02:09,899 --> 00:02:14,778
In general, the value at the end equals
the value at the start plus the integral

31
00:02:14,778 --> 00:02:15,510
of the rate.

32
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:17,581
That's the net change theorem.

33
00:02:18,699 --> 00:02:25,048
Water flows in at R of t and out at D of t
gallons per hour. The amount after six

34
00:02:25,048 --> 00:02:29,750
hours is the starting amount plus the
integral of R minus D.

35
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:33,741
The amount is increasing whenever R is
greater than D.

36
00:02:34,866 --> 00:02:41,444
Your turn. A particle has v of t equals
three t squared, and s of zero equals two.

37
00:02:41,444 --> 00:02:42,567
Find s of two.

38
00:02:43,017 --> 00:02:44,553
Pause the video and try it.

39
00:02:45,003 --> 00:02:50,682
Two plus the integral of three t squared
from zero to two, which is two plus eight:

40
00:02:50,682 --> 00:02:50,955
ten.

41
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:56,623
In this lesson, we'll find the area of a
region trapped between two curves.

42
00:02:57,767 --> 00:03:04,070
The area between f on top and g on the
bottom, from a to b, is the integral of f

43
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:04,700
minus g.

44
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:08,265
Each thin rectangle has height top minus
bottom.

45
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:13,880
Often the limits are where the curves
intersect. Set them equal and solve.

46
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:20,431
For y equals x and y equals x squared: x
equals x squared at zero and one.

47
00:03:21,567 --> 00:03:24,255
On zero to one, x is above x squared.

48
00:03:24,705 --> 00:03:30,129
The area is the integral of x minus x
squared, which is one half minus one

49
00:03:30,129 --> 00:03:31,595
third, or one sixth.

50
00:03:32,733 --> 00:03:37,984
If the curves cross, split the integral at
each crossing so that you always subtract

51
00:03:37,984 --> 00:03:38,984
bottom from top.

52
00:03:39,434 --> 00:03:42,357
Or integrate the absolute value of the
difference.

53
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:48,855
Sometimes it's easier to integrate with
respect to y, using right minus left.

54
00:03:49,305 --> 00:03:55,555
Between x equals y squared and x equals y
plus two: they meet at y equals negative

55
00:03:55,555 --> 00:04:01,806
one and two. The area is the integral of y
plus two minus y squared, which is nine

56
00:04:01,806 --> 00:04:02,339
halves.

57
00:04:03,467 --> 00:04:09,269
Your turn. Find the area between y equals
four minus x squared and the x axis.

58
00:04:09,719 --> 00:04:11,255
Pause the video and try it.

59
00:04:11,705 --> 00:04:17,337
From negative two to two, the integral of
four minus x squared is thirty two thirds.

60
00:04:18,466 --> 00:04:23,757
In this lesson, we'll find volumes of
solids by slicing them into thin pieces.

61
00:04:24,899 --> 00:04:30,928
If every slice perpendicular to the x axis
has area A of x, the volume is the

62
00:04:30,928 --> 00:04:32,729
integral of A of x d x.

63
00:04:33,179 --> 00:04:37,189
Each slice is a thin slab of volume A of x
times d x.

64
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:44,517
A solid has base between y equals root x
and the x axis from zero to four. Cross

65
00:04:44,517 --> 00:04:46,141
sections are squares.

66
00:04:46,591 --> 00:04:52,518
The side is root x, so the area is x. The
volume is the integral from zero to four

67
00:04:52,518 --> 00:04:54,036
of x, which is eight.

68
00:04:55,166 --> 00:04:59,731
Same base, but the cross sections are
semicircles with diameter root x.

69
00:05:00,181 --> 00:05:06,827
The area is one half pi times root x over
two squared, which is pi x over eight. The

70
00:05:06,827 --> 00:05:10,229
volume is pi over eight times eight, or
pi.

71
00:05:11,366 --> 00:05:17,119
For equilateral triangles with side s, the
area is root three over four times s

72
00:05:17,119 --> 00:05:17,702
squared.

73
00:05:18,152 --> 00:05:23,634
With side root x, that's root three over
four times x, and the volume is two root

74
00:05:23,634 --> 00:05:24,040
three.

75
00:05:25,166 --> 00:05:30,371
The recipe: sketch the base, find the
length of a slice in terms of x, plug into

76
00:05:30,371 --> 00:05:32,974
the shape's area formula, and integrate.

77
00:05:33,424 --> 00:05:37,669
The same idea works for slices
perpendicular to the y axis.

78
00:05:38,799 --> 00:05:44,587
Your turn. The base is the region under y
equals two from zero to three, and cross

79
00:05:44,587 --> 00:05:47,269
sections are squares. Find the volume.

80
00:05:47,719 --> 00:05:49,255
Pause the video and try it.

81
00:05:49,705 --> 00:05:52,905
Each square has area four, so the volume
is twelve.

82
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:59,594
In this lesson, we'll find the volume of a
solid formed by spinning a region around a

83
00:05:59,594 --> 00:05:59,921
line.

84
00:06:01,066 --> 00:06:06,528
Rotate a region around an axis and it
sweeps out a solid. Each slice is a disk.

85
00:06:06,978 --> 00:06:12,460
A disk of radius r and thickness d x has
volume pi r squared d x.

86
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:19,398
The disk method: V equals pi times the
integral of R of x squared d x, where R is

87
00:06:19,398 --> 00:06:22,261
the distance from the axis to the curve.

88
00:06:22,711 --> 00:06:28,911
Rotating y equals root x around the x axis
from zero to four gives pi times the

89
00:06:28,911 --> 00:06:31,500
integral of x, which is eight pi.

90
00:06:32,633 --> 00:06:37,582
Rotate y equals x squared, from zero to
two, around the x axis.

91
00:06:38,032 --> 00:06:44,091
V equals pi times the integral of x to the
fourth, which is thirty two pi over five.

92
00:06:45,233 --> 00:06:49,393
If there's a gap between the region and
the axis, slices are washers.

93
00:06:49,843 --> 00:06:55,782
V equals pi times the integral of R
squared minus r squared. Between y equals

94
00:06:55,782 --> 00:07:01,875
x and y equals x squared, around the x
axis: pi times the integral of x squared

95
00:07:01,875 --> 00:07:05,886
minus x to the fourth, which is two pi
over fifteen.

96
00:07:06,033 --> 00:07:10,276
For a different axis, measure radii from
that axis.

97
00:07:10,726 --> 00:07:16,625
Rotating around y equals negative one, the
outer radius becomes the top curve plus

98
00:07:16,625 --> 00:07:16,912
one.

99
00:07:17,033 --> 00:07:23,939
Your turn. Rotate y equals two from zero
to three around the x axis. Find the

100
00:07:23,939 --> 00:07:24,476
volume.

101
00:07:24,926 --> 00:07:26,462
Pause the video and try it.

102
00:07:26,912 --> 00:07:30,752
A cylinder: pi times four times three,
which is twelve pi.

103
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:35,889
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll find the
length of a curve.

104
00:07:37,033 --> 00:07:43,385
The length of y equals f of x from a to b
is the integral of the square root of one

105
00:07:43,385 --> 00:07:45,375
plus f prime of x squared.

106
00:07:45,825 --> 00:07:47,937
It adds up tiny hypotenuses.

107
00:07:49,067 --> 00:07:54,699
A tiny piece of curve has horizontal
change d x and vertical change d y.

108
00:07:55,149 --> 00:08:01,015
Its length is the square root of d x
squared plus d y squared, which is the

109
00:08:01,015 --> 00:08:05,005
square root of one plus d y d x squared,
times d x.

110
00:08:06,133 --> 00:08:11,445
Find the length of y equals two thirds x
to the three halves from zero to three.

111
00:08:11,895 --> 00:08:18,022
y prime is root x, so the integrand is the
square root of one plus x. The integral is

112
00:08:18,022 --> 00:08:23,716
two thirds times one plus x to the three
halves, from zero to three: two thirds

113
00:08:23,716 --> 00:08:26,743
times eight minus one, or fourteen thirds.

114
00:08:27,867 --> 00:08:30,768
Most arc length integrals can't be done by
hand.

115
00:08:31,218 --> 00:08:37,771
The length of y equals x squared from zero
to one needs a calculator: about one point

116
00:08:37,771 --> 00:08:39,005
four seven nine.

117
00:08:40,133 --> 00:08:46,320
For parametric curves, the formula becomes
the integral of the square root of x prime

118
00:08:46,320 --> 00:08:48,795
squared plus y prime squared, d t.

119
00:08:49,245 --> 00:08:50,759
We'll use it in Unit 9.

120
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:57,617
Your turn. Set up the arc length integral
for y equals sine x from zero to pi.

121
00:08:58,067 --> 00:08:59,603
Pause the video and try it.

122
00:09:00,053 --> 00:09:05,408
The integral from zero to pi of the square
root of one plus cosine squared x.

